#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198076 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "6c5e3d93-e244-52da-845b-365bb94bd19d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12562" :question/problem "Determine the quadratic Taylor polynomial for the function of two variables \n$f(x,y) = \\cos(x+y)$\nabout the point $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y) = (0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198077 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "1d13a6c0-0912-5089-81ed-c298bf747ccd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12563" :question/problem "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf\\left(x,y \\right) \n=\nx^2y-xy^3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/0d/0de380d24ed675cff86b219a2a4a337699d68e168fc875475158523022915b0c.svg) Give your answer in matrix form." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/61/61c5a4204c4ad078bd036eee7770e28faf4741bedb274a54d08d54dae43fbc68.svg) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/ab/ab531b6a5468a0ddb8cf46a42300f19cc6406c94c011ae814d60af24f66e2aa0.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/3d/3dbb0d517f6875d4a0601ba284878cb60ac0b615491564ae9458c34c0f3de403.svg)\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-{y}^{3} & {x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n![](images/3c/3cbbec95c6327e77a53bdeea5de4a93481ca9f4164ebae0cb99bf28468513a12.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198078 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "137d7caf-b232-5e21-9583-14d907a98624" :question/math-academy-id "e-12564" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/31/31305b4a4a27141bae70eabbb04acaf98928c275f8efec37ef8a869c7599a2f3.svg) to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/a7/a7a5f8961c77ad8a606ce57a543d5606b834061f3a0777b074a5711dcc9a4529.svg) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/27/276fd591c58ea0bd80eb0d3c236ade53fcd1d38864ccd650873cf052561b7586.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/06/06c98e6248f7fdb303c04772921b0423f8e2f88450d330c295bc3a72846ef858.svg)\n\nIn this case, we have ![](images/3a/3ac30d1603a502de1404a1888f7c410d22ae93b8b47db6694734a09634a43954.svg) and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n![](images/af/afd14fa251e88edb40ea71b70c56db5a03ec83d906608930e2b3ba3ba9c9fb0b.svg)\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at ![](images/1a/1af733b173aa4a358cd8c0b5c2754431be57887e7c43b0aa98125deefdecc4ad.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48adeec5-fd51-5fd0-9cbb-865947abcfc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432346} {:answer/id #uuid "603d35dd-8efa-5544-b92b-f29e859ccbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432348} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fee454-4dd2-565f-bb9f-e0034cd35e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432356} {:answer/id #uuid "c0760b03-d85d-5f8d-87d7-29831c845c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432358} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bba0dc-736f-564b-a952-443458426144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0760b03-d85d-5f8d-87d7-29831c845c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432358} :answer-field/id #uuid "d001a817-7196-53c1-ab12-d39da924742e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d7c182a-d8d9-5d67-83bd-5c70deb44da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162947" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}+{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & ∗ \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y+{y}^{3} & {x}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}+2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}6xy & 3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & 6xy+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-1)+3+(x-1)=2x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0bff6f-0cba-576c-986b-08ab8bbdc390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432335} {:answer/id #uuid "0da91aa6-2f40-536a-ae00-3ee7ffd4d87d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432336} {:answer/id #uuid "17de138d-5e8b-560d-a4fa-4ed404c20064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432339} {:answer/id #uuid "b80b9899-ed0c-5836-8d4e-76d835d659d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432357} {:answer/id #uuid "f54a1bd4-2b10-5f30-98be-179b2644a368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f54a1bd4-2b10-5f30-98be-179b2644a368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432362} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d4a3f01-2b0d-5796-9a90-ab6144e4423d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "046315b8-b9f7-5255-a5f6-6b97504a6e58" :question/math-academy-id "q-163094" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{x+y+1},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2+ax+by-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+cxy-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y+1}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y+1}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot xy+(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+1x+1y-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2})xy-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b8dc7b-9ca0-57e4-95cf-ce295303bd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432337} {:answer/id #uuid "426ee39a-6753-5cfc-b697-f79544216277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432344} {:answer/id #uuid "614bbaef-7919-554f-80d5-216b1c9e41ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432349} {:answer/id #uuid "976864ab-946d-5ed5-a93b-3f163e2eea69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432352} {:answer/id #uuid "f88f206b-7ef5-5e41-82c9-dd42ea25469f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f88f206b-7ef5-5e41-82c9-dd42ea25469f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432363} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d73795-71d5-5b5f-aef4-f1f6186ed7de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2c36de6-e7f2-52de-808d-79c266cdf722" :question/math-academy-id "q-163109" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y+1},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=1+ax-y+{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+(-1)\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-1)x-y+{x}^{2}+2xy+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-1+2+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1655fe4a-7777-5f50-b20e-15de50423a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432338} {:answer/id #uuid "2b681e68-e901-5719-ac47-b49d4ce4701f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432341} {:answer/id #uuid "7d832a86-9407-57b1-9b8a-27976545a36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432351} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e42428-9e42-5652-97ed-add0db5753d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432354} {:answer/id #uuid "feef8743-284c-59a2-9f3c-29febdcbdf54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1655fe4a-7777-5f50-b20e-15de50423a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432338} :answer-field/id #uuid "67008e00-3fcb-5aa6-b40b-a1c4a9012f7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c474ae22-f722-549c-93e7-7a344f22e1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-163127" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3x+2y+{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}5 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}3+2x{y}^{3} & 2+3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{3} & 6x{y}^{2} \\\\ 6x{y}^{2} & 6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈6+[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n6+5+(x-1)+(x-1)+6+(x-1)=3x+14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193ecd87-052b-5366-b53d-f4fee3109be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432340} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc4ca25-f9ab-58f2-a71f-3c12f6fe8a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432347} {:answer/id #uuid "7820c9d8-f18b-54a7-b0f6-12cdd2dda51a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432350} {:answer/id #uuid "9df1526a-c9df-5b82-9e9f-cdc5b44e9599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432353} {:answer/id #uuid "e7cc9897-e991-5a5c-9fd2-252b1cef639e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "193ecd87-052b-5366-b53d-f4fee3109be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432340} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb6cb031-bae3-5718-a823-8bb4cf052480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86a1a7c8-4805-516c-8e49-de078c8ddcd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-164829" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,0) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(-1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(-1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x+1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,1) & ≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2+1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-2+1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2+1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(-4) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32b5cfc5-5b79-5869-9762-17001e7c7019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432342} {:answer/id #uuid "41ed0217-d425-5f4e-ba99-e0ac900da7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432343} {:answer/id #uuid "46fa0700-2e70-5cfa-8b00-54b4c7338732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432345} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9aead5-698e-55fd-a7ed-c0810fdc4083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432355} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ec6153-7a5a-564c-b65e-6663c5889657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46fa0700-2e70-5cfa-8b00-54b4c7338732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432345} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ace458-f233-535c-b685-fcd05334410a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e65d6e32-4824-5250-9bff-fdfee93e977a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164860" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-1,-1) & =4, & \\,{f}^{′}(2,-1,-1) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(2,-1,-1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =4, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈4+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & ≈4+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5+\\frac{1}{2}(-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
